http.library/CloseRequest                            http.library/CloseRequest

   NAME
	CloseRequest -- Ends a request: its connection goes back to the client for the next request when it can, and the request is freed.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn CloseRequest(base: *HttpBase, request: ?*http.Request) void

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -32.

   INPUTS
	- `request`: from OpenRequest, or null.

   RESULT
	None.

   BEHAVIOR
	A request whose answer was read to its end, from a server that keeps
	the connection, gives the connection back to its client, which keeps
	up to four idle and closes the one idle longest to make room. Any
	other request closes its connection: one whose body was not read to
	the end, one that failed, one whose server said `Connection: close`
	or whose body ended with the connection. Null, or a pointer
	OpenRequest did not make, does nothing.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: yes, while a TLS session says goodbye.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: no spinlock may be held.
	- Process: the task that opened the client's socket base.

   OWNERSHIP
	The request is gone, and with it the strings GetRequestAttr and
	GetHeader handed out.

   NOTES
	To keep the connection of an answer whose body is of no interest, read
	the body to its end first.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`OpenRequest`, `ReadRequest`, `DeleteClient`

   EXAMPLES
	    hb.CloseRequest(request);
http.library/CreateClient                            http.library/CreateClient

   NAME
	CreateClient -- Makes a client, through which a program makes its requests and which keeps their connections for the next.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn CreateClient(base: *HttpBase, socket_base: *SocketBase, tags: ?[*]const utility.TagItem) ?*http.Client

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -20.

   INPUTS
	- `socket_base`: the program's own bsdsocket.library base, which the
	  client's connections are made in.
	- `tags`:
	  - HTTP_Verify (bool, true): the servers' certificates are checked.
	  - HTTP_UserAgent (`[*:0]const u8`): what the requests send as their
	    User-Agent; `PowerOS-http.library/1.0` unless given.
	  - HTTP_Timeout (u32, 0): the seconds a connection may stay quiet
	    before a request fails; 0 waits for as long as it takes.

   RESULT
	The client; null when there is no memory for it.

   BEHAVIOR
	Allocates the client and keeps the settings. No connection is made
	until the first request.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do; its requests need the task that opened
	  `socket_base`.

   OWNERSHIP
	The client is the caller's until DeleteClient. `socket_base` stays
	the caller's, and open until the client is deleted. The User-Agent is
	copied.

   NOTES
	A User-Agent is cut at its 95th character, and before anything that
	would end a header line.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`DeleteClient`, `OpenRequest`

   EXAMPLES
	    const client = hb.CreateClient(sb, &[_]utility.TagItem{
	        .{ .tag = http.HTTP_Timeout, .data = 30 },
	        .{},
	    }) orelse return;
	    defer hb.DeleteClient(client);
http.library/DeleteClient                            http.library/DeleteClient

   NAME
	DeleteClient -- Closes the connections a client keeps and frees the client.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn DeleteClient(base: *HttpBase, client: ?*http.Client) void

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -24.

   INPUTS
	- `client`: from CreateClient, or null.

   RESULT
	None.

   BEHAVIOR
	Closes every idle connection - a TLS session's goodbye sent first -
	and frees the client. Null, or a pointer CreateClient did not make,
	does nothing.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: yes, while a TLS session says goodbye.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: no spinlock may be held.
	- Process: the task that opened the client's socket base.

   OWNERSHIP
	The client is gone; its socket base stays the caller's.

   NOTES
	Every request made through the client must have been closed first.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`CreateClient`, `CloseRequest`

   EXAMPLES
	    hb.DeleteClient(client);
http.library/GetHeader                                  http.library/GetHeader

   NAME
	GetHeader -- Finds a header of the answer by its name and answers its value.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn GetHeader(base: *HttpBase, request: *http.Request, name: [*:0]const u8) ?[*:0]const u8

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -48.

   INPUTS
	- `request`: from OpenRequest, its answer's head read.
	- `name`: the header's name, without its colon; case does not matter.

   RESULT
	The value, without the blanks round it; null when the answer has no
	such header, while the body is still being written, or for a request
	OpenRequest did not make.

   BEHAVIOR
	Looks through the head of the answer as it came - the last answer,
	after the redirects - for the first line of that name.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The value is the request's, there until CloseRequest.

   NOTES
	Of a header given more than once (Set-Cookie) only the first is
	answered.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`GetRequestAttr`, `OpenRequest`

   EXAMPLES
	    const kind = hb.GetHeader(request, "Content-Type") orelse "application/octet-stream";
http.library/GetRequestAttr                        http.library/GetRequestAttr

   NAME
	GetRequestAttr -- Answers one of a request's attributes.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn GetRequestAttr(base: *HttpBase, request: *http.Request, attr: utility.Tag) usize

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -44.

   INPUTS
	- `request`: from OpenRequest.
	- `attr`:
	  - HTTP_Status: the answer's status, 200 for OK; 0 while the body is
	    being written.
	  - HTTP_StatusLine (`[*:0]const u8`): `HTTP/1.1 404 Not Found`; empty
	    while the body is being written.
	  - HTTP_ContentLength: the body's length as the answer gives it, or
	    HTTP_LENGTH_UNKNOWN for one in chunks, one that ends with the
	    connection, or one being unpacked.
	  - HTTP_URL (`[*:0]const u8`): the URL that answered, after the
	    redirects.
	  - HTTP_Reused (bool): the request went over a connection an earlier
	    one had made.
	  - HTTP_Secure (bool): it went over TLS.
	  - HTTP_Error: the HTTPERR_* that broke it, or HTTPERR_OK.
	  - HTTP_Verdict: the certificate check's TLSV_*, over TLS.
	  - HTTP_Unzipping (bool): ReadRequest unpacks the body.

   RESULT
	The attribute's value; 0 for an attribute there is not, or a request
	OpenRequest did not make.

   BEHAVIOR
	Reads the request; nothing goes over the connection.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	A string is the request's, there until CloseRequest.

   NOTES
	On a 32-bit chip a length of 4 GiB or more reads as
	HTTP_LENGTH_UNKNOWN; the Content-Length header has it.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`GetHeader`, `OpenRequest`

   EXAMPLES
	    const status = hb.GetRequestAttr(request, http.HTTP_Status);
	    const line: [*:0]const u8 = @ptrFromInt(hb.GetRequestAttr(request, http.HTTP_StatusLine));
http.library/OpenRequest                              http.library/OpenRequest

   NAME
	OpenRequest -- Sends a request to a URL and reads the head of its answer, following redirects.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn OpenRequest(base: *HttpBase, client: *http.Client, method: [*:0]const u8, url: [*:0]const u8, tags: ?[*]const utility.TagItem, err: ?*i32) ?*http.Request

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -28.

   INPUTS
	- `client`: from CreateClient.
	- `method`: `GET`, `POST`, `HEAD`, `PUT`, ... - at most 15 characters.
	- `url`: `http://host[:port][/path][?query]` or `https://...`, the host
	  a name, an IPv4 address or an IPv6 one in brackets; at most 2048
	  characters. A `#fragment` is not sent.
	- `tags`:
	  - HTTP_Header (`[*:0]const u8`, as often as needed): a header line
	    of the program's own, `Name: value`.
	  - HTTP_Body (`*const anyopaque`) and HTTP_BodyLength (u32): a body
	    in memory, sent with the head.
	  - HTTP_BodyLength alone: a body of that many bytes the program
	    writes with WriteRequest.
	  - HTTP_Chunked (bool, false): a body the program writes with
	    WriteRequest, of a length not known in advance.
	  - HTTP_User and HTTP_Password (`[*:0]const u8`): Basic
	    authorization, at most 2047 characters the two together; a token
	    goes as the password.
	  - HTTP_Redirects (u32, 5): the most redirects followed; 0 makes a
	    redirect the answer.
	  - HTTP_Unzip (bool, false): the server may pack the body, and
	    ReadRequest unpacks it.
	  - HTTP_GetTLSError (`*i32`), HTTP_GetVerdict (`*u32`), HTTP_GetAlert
	    (`*u32`): where the TLS session's TLSERR_*, its certificate
	    check's TLSV_* and the alert a server refused with go.
	- `err`: where the outcome goes (HTTPERR_*), or null.

   RESULT
	The request, its answer's head read - or, with a body the program
	writes, its own head sent - and `err` HTTPERR_OK. Null, with `err`:
	HTTPERR_URL for a URL that is none, HTTPERR_HOST for a host without
	an address, HTTPERR_IO when no connection could be made or it failed,
	HTTPERR_TLS when the TLS session could not be had, HTTPERR_PROTOCOL
	for an answer that is not HTTP, HTTPERR_TRUNCATED for one cut short,
	HTTPERR_REDIRECT for too many redirects or one to nowhere,
	HTTPERR_STATE for a method or header that is none, credentials too
	long, or a request head longer than 8 KiB, HTTPERR_LIBRARY without tls.library for
	`https://` or compression.library for HTTP_Unzip, HTTPERR_DATA for a
	packed answer that does not start as one, HTTPERR_NOMEM.

	An answer is an answer whatever its status: a 404 or a 500 is a
	request whose HTTP_Status says so.

   BEHAVIOR
	The request goes as HTTP/1.1 over a connection the client kept to the
	same scheme, host and port, or a new one - over TLS for `https://`,
	asking for `http/1.1`. Its head carries Host, User-Agent, the Basic
	authorization, `Accept-Encoding: gzip, deflate` with HTTP_Unzip, the
	program's headers, and the body's Content-Length - or
	`Transfer-Encoding: chunked` for HTTP_Chunked. A POST, PUT or PATCH
	without a body says its length is 0.

	A kept connection that fails before any of the answer has come is
	given up and the request sent again on a new one: a server may close
	a connection it has been idle on at any time. A body the program
	writes always goes over a new connection.

	Interim answers (100 Continue) are read past. A redirect - 301, 302,
	303, 307 or 308 with a Location - is followed when the request's body
	is not the program's to write: its body read and dropped, and the
	request sent to where it leads. 303 asks with GET, and 301 and 302
	turn a POST into a GET, both without the body; 307 and 308 keep the
	method and the body. A redirect to another scheme, host or port goes
	without HTTP_User's authorization and without the program's
	Authorization and Cookie headers.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: yes, for the server, as long as HTTP_Timeout allows; Ctrl-C
	  ends a wait with HTTPERR_IO.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: no spinlock may be held.
	- Process: yes, the one that opened the client's socket base - the
	  host is looked up, and tls.library reads its certificate stores.

   OWNERSHIP
	The request is the caller's until CloseRequest. The strings, the
	header lines and an in-memory body need only last for the call.

   NOTES
	A request takes some 14 KiB, and 8 KiB more with HTTP_Unzip; a TLS
	connection some 75 KiB more. The answer's head is read into the
	request's 8 KiB: one longer fails with HTTPERR_PROTOCOL.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`ReadRequest`, `WriteRequest`, `GetRequestAttr`, `GetHeader`,
	`CloseRequest`, `CreateClient`

   EXAMPLES
	    var err: i32 = 0;
	    const request = hb.OpenRequest(client, "GET", "https://example.org/", &[_]utility.TagItem{
	        .{ .tag = http.HTTP_Header, .data = @intFromPtr("Accept: text/html") },
	        .{ .tag = http.HTTP_Unzip, .data = 1 },
	        .{},
	    }, &err) orelse return err;
	    defer hb.CloseRequest(request);
	    if (hb.GetRequestAttr(request, http.HTTP_Status) != 200) return;
http.library/ReadRequest                              http.library/ReadRequest

   NAME
	ReadRequest -- Reads the next bytes of the answer's body.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn ReadRequest(base: *HttpBase, request: *http.Request, buffer: *anyopaque, length: u32) i32

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -36.

   INPUTS
	- `request`: from OpenRequest, its answer's head read.
	- `buffer`: room for `length` bytes.
	- `length`: how many bytes at most.

   RESULT
	How many bytes were read, at least 1; 0 at the body's end; below 0
	an HTTPERR_*: HTTPERR_IO when the connection failed, HTTPERR_TLS when
	its session did, HTTPERR_TRUNCATED when it ended before the body,
	HTTPERR_PROTOCOL for chunks that make no sense, HTTPERR_DATA for a
	packed body that does not unpack, HTTPERR_STATE while the program's
	own body has not been ended.

   BEHAVIOR
	Hands over first what came with the head, then what the connection
	brings, as soon as it brings any: a read may answer fewer bytes than
	asked for long before the end. The body is decoded from its chunks; a
	body packed with gzip or deflate is unpacked when the request asked
	for HTTP_Unzip, and its end is the packed stream's. A body ends where
	its Content-Length says, at its last chunk, or where the server closes
	the connection; the answer to HEAD, a 204 and a 304 have none.

	An error breaks the request: every read after it answers the same
	error, and the connection is not kept.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: yes, for the server, as long as HTTP_Timeout allows; Ctrl-C
	  ends a wait with HTTPERR_IO.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: no spinlock may be held.
	- Process: the task that opened the client's socket base.

   OWNERSHIP
	The bytes are the caller's.

   NOTES
	One read takes at most 2 GiB less 64 KiB, whatever `length` says.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`OpenRequest`, `GetRequestAttr`, `CloseRequest`

   EXAMPLES
	    var buffer: [4096]u8 = undefined;
	    while (true) {
	        const got = hb.ReadRequest(request, &buffer, buffer.len);
	        if (got < 0) return got;
	        if (got == 0) break;
	        _ = dos.Write(file, &buffer, got);
	    }
http.library/WriteRequest                            http.library/WriteRequest

   NAME
	WriteRequest -- Sends the next bytes of a request's body the program writes, or ends the body and reads the head of the answer.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn WriteRequest(base: *HttpBase, request: *http.Request, data: ?*const anyopaque, length: u32) i32

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -40.

   INPUTS
	- `request`: from OpenRequest with HTTP_Chunked, or with
	  HTTP_BodyLength and no HTTP_Body.
	- `data`: the bytes; null with a `length` of 0.
	- `length`: how many; 0 ends the body.

   RESULT
	For bytes: `length` once they are all sent. For the end: the answer's
	status, once its head is read. Below 0 an HTTPERR_*: HTTPERR_STATE
	for a request whose body is not the program's, for bytes past the
	length it gave, for an end before all of them came, or for a
	`length` past 2 GiB; HTTPERR_IO, HTTPERR_TLS, HTTPERR_PROTOCOL,
	HTTPERR_TRUNCATED and HTTPERR_DATA as OpenRequest answers them.

   BEHAVIOR
	A body with HTTP_BodyLength goes as it is, and is ended once all of
	its bytes have been written; one with HTTP_Chunked goes as a chunk
	per call, of up to 8 KiB each, and its end is the last chunk. The
	answer to it is read as OpenRequest reads one, interim answers aside,
	but a redirect is not followed: it is the answer. After the end, the
	body of the answer is read with ReadRequest.

	A failure breaks the request: every call after it answers the same
	error, and the connection is not kept.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: yes, for the server, as long as HTTP_Timeout allows; Ctrl-C
	  ends a wait with HTTPERR_IO.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: no spinlock may be held.
	- Process: the task that opened the client's socket base.

   OWNERSHIP
	The bytes stay the caller's; they are sent before the call returns.

   NOTES
	A server may answer before it has the whole body - to refuse it, say
	- and close; the next WriteRequest then fails with HTTPERR_IO.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`OpenRequest`, `ReadRequest`, `CloseRequest`

   EXAMPLES
	    const request = hb.OpenRequest(client, "POST", url, &[_]utility.TagItem{
	        .{ .tag = http.HTTP_Chunked, .data = 1 },
	        .{ .tag = http.HTTP_Header, .data = @intFromPtr("Content-Type: application/octet-stream") },
	        .{},
	    }, &err) orelse return err;
	    defer hb.CloseRequest(request);
	    while (more(&piece)) {
	        if (hb.WriteRequest(request, piece.ptr, @intCast(piece.len)) < 0) return;
	    }
	    const status = hb.WriteRequest(request, null, 0);
